body.proof_tex:
Let the pooled-law tuple be given and assume \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=K\). By \(\mathrm{thm{:}rank\text{-}frontier}\), for every \(z\in(0,1]^p\),
\[
(G_1(z),\ldots,G_K(z))^{\mathsf T}
 =\mathscr R_M(H)(z)
 =\exp\{M^+\log H(z)\}. \tag{1}
\]
The same theorem's PGF-coefficient uniqueness therefore identifies each labeled observational law \(P_r\). This is the proved equality between an observable recovery functional and the component observational law; it is not a definition of either a graph or an intervention effect.

Under the corrected definition of \(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{CPCM}}\), membership of \(((P_r,D_r))_{r=1}^K\) means that each \(P_r\) is a count-valued, causally minimal, pairwise-identifiable CPCM in the sense of Definitions 9--10 of Bodik--Chavez-Demoulin. In particular, no Poisson or other parametric restriction is imposed on a source-node law. Applying the cited \(\mathrm{lem{:}cpcm\text{-}dag\text{-}identification}\) to the already recovered \(P_r\) gives a unique labeled generating DAG \(D_r\). Equation (1) keeps the types labeled because its coordinates are the columns of the known matrix \(M\). Applying the lemma separately for \(r=1,\ldots,K\) identifies the labeled graph tuple.

Now impose the second, stricter premise that the recovered tuple lies in \(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{poly}}(\mathfrak G,\Theta,q)\). By \(\mathrm{prop{:}polynomial\text{-}class\text{-}inclusion}\), this model has full support on \(\mathbb N_0^p\), is causally sufficient, and lies in \(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{CPCM}}\). Thus its graph has already been identified by the preceding paragraph. Full support implies, for every node \(s\), type \(r\), and parent configuration \(v_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}\),
\[
\Pr_{P_r}\!\left(
 V_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
 =v_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
\right)>0. \tag{2}
\]
Consequently the observational conditional kernel is the identified ratio
\[
p_{rs,\theta}\!\left(v_s\mid
v_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}\right)
=
\frac{
 \Pr_{P_r}\!\left(
 V_s=v_s,\,
 V_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
 =v_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
 \right)}
{\Pr_{P_r}\!\left(
 V_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
 =v_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
 \right)}. \tag{3}
\]
For a root, the denominator in (3) is \(1\). The division in (3) is justified by (2); this is the overlap step used by the intervention functional.

We next prove that the truncated product equals the causal post-intervention law. Causal sufficiency supplies mutually independent exogenous variables for the acyclic structural assignments, and intervention modularity replaces only the assignment for \(V_{i_X}\) by the constant \(x\in\{1,2\}\). Generate the remaining variables in a topological order of \(D_r\). Independence of the exogenous variables and the defining conditional kernels then give, by the chain rule,
\[
\Pr\!\left(
 V_{-i_X}=v_{-i_X}
 \ \middle|\ 
 \operatorname{do}(V_{i_X}=x)
\right)
=
\prod_{s\ne i_X}
p_{rs,\theta}\!\left(
 v_s\mid v_{\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
\right)\Big|_{v_{i_X}=x}. \tag{4}
\]
This also verifies that the displayed functional is a probability law. Indeed, all factors in (4) are nonnegative, so the multiple series is the supremum of its finite rectangular partial sums and may be evaluated by iterated summation. Sum in reverse topological order over \(v_s\) for \(s\ne i_X\); at each step the only remaining factor involving the current terminal variable is its conditional mass function, whose sum is \(1\). The total mass is therefore \(1\). Thus (4) is derived from the structural intervention and is not postulated as the causal estimand.

It remains to verify that the expectations defining the two contrasts exist. If \(N\sim\operatorname{Pois}(\lambda)\), then direct summation gives, for every integer \(\ell\geq0\),
\[
\mathbb E[(N)_\ell]
 =\sum_{n=\ell}^{\infty}
 \frac{n!}{(n-\ell)!}e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^n}{n!}
 =\lambda^\ell, \tag{5}
\]
where \((N)_\ell=N(N-1)\cdots(N-\ell+1)\). Expanding \(N^m\) as a finite linear combination of \((N)_0,\ldots,(N)_m\) shows that every Poisson moment is a finite polynomial in \(\lambda\). Under either fixed intervention, \(V_{i_X}=x\) has moments of every order. Proceeding in topological order, roots have finite moments by (5). If all parents of a non-root \(s\) have moments of every order, then its rate
\[
\lambda_{rs,\theta}
 =\alpha_{rs}+
 \sum_{k\in\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
 b_{rsk}V_k^{q_{sk}} \tag{6}
\]
is a finite positive polynomial in those parents. More explicitly, if \(h=1+|\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)|\), elementary convexity gives for every integer \(m\geq1\)
\[
\lambda_{rs,\theta}^{m}
\leq h^{m-1}\left(
\alpha_{rs}^{m}+
\sum_{k\in\operatorname{pa}_{D_r}(s)}
b_{rsk}^{m}V_k^{m q_{sk}}
\right). \tag{7}
\]
The induction hypothesis makes the expectation of the right-hand side finite. Equations (5) and (7) then show that every conditional moment of \(V_s\), and hence every unconditional moment of \(V_s\), is finite. Acyclic induction proves this for all nonintervened nodes. In particular, the first moments of \(V_{i_Y}\) and \(V_{i_Z}\) under \(x=1,2\) are finite.

Finally, substitute the identified kernels (3) into (4), multiply by \((v_{i_Y},v_{i_Z})\), sum over \(v_{-i_X}\), and subtract the result at \(x=1\) from that at \(x=2\). By (4) and the finiteness just proved, this observable functional equals, component by component, the causal vector \(\boldsymbol\tau=((\tau_{i_Y,r},\tau_{i_Z,r}))_{r=1}^K\) in \(\mathrm{def{:}truncated\text{-}factorization\text{-}effect}\). Hence the pooled laws identify the claimed intervention contrasts only on the strict polynomial subclass, while labeled-DAG recovery holds on the broader published CPCM class.
